# UML Notation Guardrails

Use standard UML semantics.

## Relationships
- Association: structural relationship between classifiers; use when objects are meaningfully connected.
- Dependency: one element uses or depends on another without implying structural ownership.
- Generalization: an "is-a" relationship with substitutability.
- Realization: a classifier fulfills a contract such as an interface.
- Aggregation: use only when shared/whole-part semantics are genuinely meaningful; do not use it as a generic "has-a" arrow.
- Composition: use only when the part has strong lifecycle ownership by the whole and the intended lifecycle semantics are true.

## Multiplicity
Use explicit multiplicities when they matter to the business model. Do not invent `1..*` or `0..1` without evidence.

## Names
- Use domain language rather than UI labels when modeling the domain.
- Use nouns for classes/entities and meaningful verb phrases for operations/use cases.
- Avoid vague classes such as `Gestion`, `Systeme`, `Manager`, or `Data` unless their responsibility is justified.

## Common mistakes to reject
- Every class connected to every other class.
- Composition used for every containment relation.
- Inheritance used only to reuse fields.
- Database tables copied blindly into a conceptual class diagram.
- Buttons/screens treated as domain classes.
- Use cases named after implementation methods.
- Sequence diagrams that contain no meaningful responsibility or decision logic.
